WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.930
Hello, everyone, and welcome to our second drink of Boys and Girls.

2
00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:12.530
This episode is near and dear to our hearts because we got to have all the ladies in scenes together all day.

3
00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:16.550
And this episode was directed by the legendary Denny Gordon.

4
00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:17.710
Yes.

5
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:24.730
And, you know, Boys and Girls is on Dave Rogers' list of favorite Peacock superfan episodes.

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:25.350
Yep.

7
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:29.010
So we actually reached out to the director of Boys and Girls, Denny Gordon,

8
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:34.610
and we are going to have her on Office Ladies as our special guest to talk all about the superfan episode.

9
00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.090
I really can't wait for it, and we'll let you know when that's coming out.

10
00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.790
So we aren't going to give any new insights on this episode today.

11
00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:43.170
You'll have to wait for our superfan breakdown for that.

12
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.910
But we didn't want to leave you hanging, so I thought we could answer a question from our general mailbox.

13
00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:48.750
Great.

14
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:50.810
This came in from Avery C.

15
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.990
in Richmond, Virginia, who said,

16
00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:54.710
Hello, Jenna and Angela.

17
00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.430
I would love to know about rehearsals.

18
00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.910
When did you do rehearsals?

19
00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.710
How did they fit in the general schedule of the week?

20
00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:05.130
Filming, getting lighting, locations?

21
00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:11.090
Did you rehearse each scene every day, or did you front load all the rehearsals at the beginning of the week?

22
00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:15.010
Did you wear your costume and makeup for rehearsals or ordinary clothes?

23
00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.510
How long did rehearsals usually take?

24
00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:21.230
Basically, Avery wants the full download on rehearsals.

25
00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.350
Well, Avery, I think this is a great question.

26
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.310
You know, every single show or movie you work on is a little different.

27
00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:32.590
And our show, we typically did a rehearsal for camera and for the actor and directors,

28
00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.930
and we weren't always in wardrobe for those.

29
00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.930
Depending on where your call time was and where you were in the mix of the day,

30
00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.110
you might be halfway through hair and makeup.

31
00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.610
You might be fully dressed because you had already been in a scene that day.

32
00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:49.630
But we would always walk through the scene for the camera crew and the lighting crew.

33
00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:54.150
Yes, and those rehearsals would happen right before we shot that scene.

34
00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:58.870
So it's not like we came in on Monday mornings and rehearsed everything in a big clump.

35
00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:03.650
Whatever scene we were getting ready to shoot, it would begin with a rehearsal.

36
00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.810
And we would all sit and sometimes even get out our scripts and read through it,

37
00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:12.890
talk about where we thought each person would stand or how they would cross or move,

38
00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.030
and play out the beats of the scene.

39
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.850
Camera would figure out how they were going to cover it.

40
00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:20.410
And that was the rehearsal.

41
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:24.230
It was not usually even done for performance.

42
00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.610
No, hardly ever done for performance.

43
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.550
Really more of a walkthrough for the crew.

44
00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:35.510
And what I always did when I had bigger scenes with a lot of dialogue is I just rehearsed like

45
00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:37.430
crazy on my own in my trailer.

46
00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:42.710
Sometimes Jenna and I would meet and run lines together or whoever you might be in a scene with.

47
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.970
Phyllis and I, I remember we would rehearse many of our big scenes where

48
00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.830
our two characters had conflict.

49
00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:55.070
Well, when you work on a television show or a movie, you are expected to do all of that work at home.

50
00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:56.150
That is your homework.

51
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:00.690
You're meant to come in prepared with your lines ready, your choices made.

52
00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:07.230
The rehearsal process on a movie or a television show is not there for you to make any acting

53
00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.710
discoveries necessarily.

54
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:14.590
And sometimes it's your very first time hearing how the other actor in the scene is doing their

55
00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.490
part, which is super weird.

56
00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:21.870
So, of course, on The Office, we got to know each other so well.

57
00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:27.650
I could just hear how Angela was going to say it or how Brian Baumgartner was going to say

58
00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:28.570
something as Kevin.

59
00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:35.190
But, you know, on other movies, sometimes you're meeting your scene partner that you're supposed

60
00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:40.130
to have this long character history with for the first time in that little rehearsal right

61
00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:40.950
before you shoot.

62
00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:41.450
Yeah.

63
00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.330
But then you do the rehearsal and then there'll be a little break.

64
00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.370
So you do get a chance to kind of download that information.

65
00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.070
And during that break, you're meant to get into your wardrobe or get your hair and makeup

66
00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:52.750
touched up.

67
00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:58.170
And then that's when the lighting crew will come in and they'll make sure that everything's

68
00:03:58.170 --> 00:03:58.490
ready.

69
00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.790
And that's about a 30-minute break on our set, sometimes less.

70
00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:03.410
Yeah.

71
00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:08.650
And sometimes the physicality of the scene doesn't end up being the way you thought it was going

72
00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:08.950
to be.

73
00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:14.170
I remember there was a scene from accounting where I interject into a conversation that's

74
00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:15.090
happening in the bullpen.

75
00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:19.910
And when I read it, I thought, oh, I'd probably get up and I walk over to the bullpen.

76
00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:27.070
But the way they were framing the wide shot, Randall Einhorn had me yell my line from accounting.

77
00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:28.950
And that sort of changed how I said it.

78
00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:29.570
For sure.

79
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.410
It had a bigger presence in the room.

80
00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.990
So you kind of have to have some flexibility in how you rehearsed it on your own at home

81
00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:38.850
into how it's going to end up being that day on set.

82
00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:39.230
Yeah.

83
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.130
Well, Avery, thank you for that question.

84
00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:41.870
Yes.

85
00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.450
Thank you so much for writing in.

86
00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:49.050
And be sure to catch our Peacock superfan breakdown of Boys and Girls with director

87
00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:52.010
amazing Denny Gordon later this year.

88
00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.290
But first, here is our original breakdown of Boys and Girls.

89
00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:00.360
I'm Jenna Fischer.

90
00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:01.620
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

91
00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:02.880
We were on The Office together.

92
00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.160
And we're best friends.

93
00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.960
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

94
00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:12.320
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes

95
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.960
stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

96
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.100
We're The Office Ladies.

97
00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:19.680
Hey, gang.

98
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.620
Hello, everybody.

99
00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:23.480
I'm wearing my Office Ladies hoodie today.

100
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:24.460
You really are.

101
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:26.200
I am holding a note card.

102
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:27.800
It's almost like I want to play it.

103
00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:28.880
I'm like in a poker game.

104
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.280
I'm like ready to drop it.

105
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:30.720
It's your ace of spades.

106
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:31.760
Drop it like it's hot.

107
00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:32.660
Drop it like it's hot.

108
00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:34.420
But it says Angela Sass.

109
00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:35.220
It does.

110
00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:38.680
And there's a lot of Angela Sass in this episode.

111
00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.700
So you're ready to play that Sass.

112
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.520
I'm ready because I know you have your stuff you're going to say.

113
00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.220
But I'm holding an Angela Sass.

114
00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.360
It's coming in hot.

115
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.360
Today, we're talking about Boys and Girls.

116
00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:56.200
Season 2, episode 15, written by BJ Novak and directed by Denny Gordon.

117
00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.440
Badass Denny Gordon.

118
00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:58.400
Yeah, she's back.

119
00:05:58.600 --> 00:05:59.700
Should I read a summary?

120
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:00.400
Do it.

121
00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:00.940
All right.

122
00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:06.620
Jan hosts a Women in the Workplace seminar with the women of The Office.

123
00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:08.240
But Michael feels threatened.

124
00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:14.360
And so he decides to throw a competing Men in the Workplace seminar down in the warehouse,

125
00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.520
which he slowly destroys.

126
00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.140
Fast fact number one, fan question from Dan Felve.

127
00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:26.840
Was the idea for this episode based on anything that anyone went through, like in a previous job?

128
00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:27.840
No, Dan.

129
00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.820
It's based on an idea that Angela and I pitched.

130
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.140
That's right, Dan.

131
00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:32.450
Woo!

132
00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:33.220
Dan.

133
00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.300
This is our proudest moment.

134
00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:35.260
We love this.

135
00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:35.880
We love this.

136
00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.500
Also, we love that Greg Daniels was so collaborative, right?

137
00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:39.920
Yeah.

138
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.380
That we could have this idea.

139
00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:44.240
So, you know, Jen and I both worked in corporate America.

140
00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.320
And so we both had to go to a lot of like human resource meetings and things like that.

141
00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.980
And we were on set one day sort of talking about it.

142
00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.740
And I wrote about it in my journal.

143
00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.280
Lady, I did too.

144
00:06:55.460 --> 00:06:58.380
I wrote on Thursday, February 2nd, 2006,

145
00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.800
Boys and Girls, there are many things I love about working on this show.

146
00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:04.540
But one of the coolest is the creative input that we get to have.

147
00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:07.300
A lot of shows just don't let their actors get involved that way.

148
00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:12.440
But Jen and I were talking about our lives before the show, when she was a secretary and I was an operator.

149
00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:18.380
And we were laughing about all the crazy seminars and ridiculous meetings we had to sit through at our old corporate jobs.

150
00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:22.340
We sort of had this idea that it would be a funny episode called Women in the Workplace.

151
00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.100
We ran into Greg Daniels and told him our little idea.

152
00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:27.560
We didn't have anything fleshed out or written down.

153
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:28.940
And he got so excited.

154
00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:31.320
He grabbed BJ and he said, start writing this down.

155
00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.940
And BJ got a notepad and started jotting things down.

156
00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:35.920
Greg started scribbling notes too.

157
00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.700
Weeks later, BJ Novak wrote this awesome script.

158
00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.300
Jen and I were so excited.

159
00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.940
We are so lucky to be a part of a show that would respect our ideas.

160
00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.040
Oh, that is so nice.

161
00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:49.400
You wrote mostly about the collaboration.

162
00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:52.320
I wrote very differently about this in my journal.

163
00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.800
Wait, can I say the last thing I wrote was,

164
00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:56.560
to any of the writers out there,

165
00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.140
we're not going to get in the habit of just bugging you guys with ideas.

166
00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:00.680
I know.

167
00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.000
We would always have these bright ideas as a cast,

168
00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.920
and we always worried that we annoyed the writers.

169
00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.160
But they were always so receptive and nice.

170
00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:07.600
I know.

171
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:08.480
Even if they were pretending.

172
00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:10.180
Okay, so what did you write about?

173
00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:17.440
Okay, I went on a very elaborate description of where we were when we came up with the idea for this episode.

174
00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:18.200
Where were we?

175
00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.220
We were in my trailer.

176
00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:21.840
We were having a picnic lunch.

177
00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:22.640
Oh my gosh.

178
00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.580
I described that we had scented candles.

179
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:32.220
I love our two different accounts of this moment in time.

180
00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.700
Yes, scented candles, and that we were discussing it.

181
00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.000
And I also wrote that we did this all the time.

182
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.360
We would have these ladies' lunches.

183
00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:47.140
And during this ladies' lunch, we got the bright idea for this episode that we ran onto the set,

184
00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.480
that we set it to Greg Daniels, same as you.

185
00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:54.740
He pulled BJ aside, and BJ's like, oh my gosh, what is this episode idea?

186
00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.680
And we said, here's what it is.

187
00:08:56.860 --> 00:09:01.620
The idea is that Jan comes in and has a woman in the workplace seminar,

188
00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:06.080
and then Michael gets jealous, and he says, I want a man in the workplace seminar.

189
00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:07.740
And BJ's like, I love it.

190
00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:08.640
What happens next?

191
00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:09.540
And we're like, we don't know.

192
00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:09.900
That's it.

193
00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:10.660
That's all we got.

194
00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:12.140
He's like, that's it.

195
00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:12.740
That's the whole thing.

196
00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:13.320
We're like, yeah.

197
00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.520
And he's like, okay, I have a lot of work to do.

198
00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.720
And BJ filled in all the blanks.

199
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:19.300
All the reSt.

200
00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:27.600
Fast fact number two, another fan question from Elani Swentzel.

201
00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:34.080
One of my favorite things is watching you read people's Twitter or Instagram handles.

202
00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:39.780
Elani asked, were the scenes in the warehouse and the scenes in the conference room filmed at separate times?

203
00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.720
If so, where were the other cast members during that time?

204
00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.000
We got a lot of questions about this.

205
00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.700
People wanted to know, did we have two different film crews?

206
00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:49.820
How did this work?

207
00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:50.240
No.

208
00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:51.260
One film crew.

209
00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:56.040
There were a couple of days where all of the women came to work, and we shot our scenes.

210
00:09:56.260 --> 00:09:59.120
And then there were a couple of days where all the men came to work.

211
00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.160
I don't know what I did, but I had the day off.

212
00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:03.880
And I remember it was glorious.

213
00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:04.740
This was rare.

214
00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:05.240
Yeah.

215
00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:07.080
No, I remember being really excited.

216
00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:09.580
First of all, there was just a day where it was just all us gals.

217
00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:10.740
And it was really fun.

218
00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:12.840
I wrote about that in my diary as well.

219
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:22.300
And I think part of the inspiration for this episode came from our desire to do more scenes together and be with the other women in the office doing scenes.

220
00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:29.100
And so it was a really fun week, not just because we got to hang out with the gals, but also we did have half the week off.

221
00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:29.540
We did.

222
00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:30.300
Which was really cool.

223
00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.260
And then we had one day where we were all together.

224
00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:32.540
Yeah.

225
00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:35.720
To film the scenes where the boys and girls are all together.

226
00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:36.040
I know.

227
00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:38.300
And I remember we were kind of excited to see everybody.

228
00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:38.980
We're like, hey.

229
00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:40.140
Yeah, how's it been?

230
00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:41.360
What happened down there?

231
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:43.220
We'd been on summer vacation or something.

232
00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:44.000
Okay.

233
00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:50.420
Fast fact number three, this episode is Jim and Pam's first fight.

234
00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:51.980
It's a real fight.

235
00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:53.680
Yeah, this is a real fight.

236
00:10:53.840 --> 00:11:01.560
Now, they got into a little bit of a, what would you call it, a disagreement in the episode, the fight, when Jim picks her up at the dojo.

237
00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.360
Well, they've had a few bumpy moments.

238
00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:04.580
This is a fight.

239
00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:05.600
It's not resolved.

240
00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:07.000
We'll get into it.

241
00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:07.320
Yeah.

242
00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:11.600
But it's, yeah, they had some harsh words at each other.

243
00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:16.860
This is also another episode where Jim and Pam are basically separated the entire episode.

244
00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:20.680
So now we've had two episodes in a row where they're physically separated.

245
00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:25.860
In the last episode, you know, Pam and Roy were really getting along.

246
00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.100
